    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "A person can now be put on hold and taken off it again, and all 4 tests in the spec pass under `bin/e2e`.\n\n**What it does**\n- Each person's row shows `person-hold` (yes/no) and a `person-hold-toggle` button, labelled Hold or Release depending on the current state.\n- While someone is held, their OPEN claims are left out of every live total: their own claimed and due-total, the sheet's totals and period totals. Their SETTLED claims still count. Each claim's own due amount is still shown on its row.\n- Holding writes `PERSON_HELD \u2026 name=<name>` and releasing writes `PERSON_RELEASED \u2026 name=<name>` to the audit file. After that come the usual `ROLLUP`/`TOTALS` lines, but only when the hold actually changed a figure. So holding someone with no open claims writes just the one line, which is what the tests expect.\n- If the action is refused (for example the person doesn't exist), the row's existing `person-error` element shows, and the next action on that row clears it.\n\n**Where the changes are**\n- **Stored data:** a new migration, `V16__person_held.sql`, adds a `held` column to `person`.\n- **Server:** a new endpoint, `POST /api/sheets/{sheetId}/people/{personId}/hold`, switches the hold on or off. The rule for which claims count now lives in one place, `Person.counts(status)`. `Rollup` uses it for both the person/sheet totals and period totals, and so does closing a period, since it goes through `Rollup`.\n- **Front end:** the same counting rule is applied in `Claims.tsx`, `People.tsx` and `Periods.tsx`, because the screen also adds up the totals itself.\n\n**One decision for you:** the request didn't say what a statement should do with a held person's open claims. Since a statement records the due-total as the screen shows it, I made it leave those claims out of its total and claim count. It also leaves them as they are rather than unbinding them, so their figures are still there when the person is released. The tests don't exercise this either way, so tell me if you'd rather it treat held claims differently.",
